        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Columbia projects, from Shandon to the lake</strong></p><p>Columbia sits where the Broad and Saluda rivers meet to form the Congaree, on the fall line between the Piedmont and the Sandhills. The city's older neighborhoods, Shandon, Rosewood and Forest Acres, have bungalows and brick ranches with back decks and screened porches. A glass deck railing there opens the view onto a shaded yard or a pool. Near the University of South Carolina, renovated homes add glass stair railing on open staircases.</p><p>The big projects are on Lake Murray. Homes in Lexington, Chapin, Irmo and the coves toward Saluda County have multi-level decks facing the water. A frameless glass railing on each level keeps the lake in view from the house. Northeast of town, Lake Carolina, Blythewood and Elgin are new-build territory. Builders there use glass balcony railing on upper porches and glass guards on interior landings in place of wood balusters.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Permits and the code around Columbia</strong></p><p>South Carolina adopts its codes statewide, and the 2021 International Residential Code applies to houses in Columbia and its suburbs. The South Carolina code sets guards at 36 inches minimum for houses and 42 inches for commercial and multifamily buildings. For a pool deck, the International Swimming Pool and Spa Code, section 305, sets a 48-inch barrier. Our 46-inch panel in spigots gives that height.</p><p>Which office issues the permit depends on the address. Inside the city limits it is the City of Columbia. Unincorporated addresses go through Richland County or Lexington County. Check with your local building department about whether a railing swap or a new deck needs a permit. Our engineering documents, with testing and blocking details, are available for the file. Read our <a href=\"/pages/south-carolina-building-code-for-glass-railings\">South Carolina building code for glass railings</a> summary before you set panel heights.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>A railing for Columbia's heat and humidity</strong></p><p>Columbia once marketed itself as Famously Hot, and the summers earn it. Highs in the mid-90s are routine from June through September, with thick humidity and afternoon thunderstorms. Wood railings on a south-facing deck or a Lake Murray dock side gray out, check and need staining every couple of years. Tempered 12 mm glass and 2205 marine-grade stainless steel do not absorb water, rot or rust. Pool chlorine splashing on the spigots is not a problem either.</p><p>The glass takes direct sun all summer, and tempered safety glass does not warp or peel like painted wood. Winters are mild, with rain more than snow, so freeze-thaw is rarely a factor here. Spring brings pine pollen that films every surface in the Midlands. On glass it rinses off with a hose in minutes, with no pickets to scrub between.</p>"
          }
        }
